The main cost is repeated work per call, so this version cuts that down while returning the same values:

- **Single type lookup:** `GetType()` is called once and the result is reused.
- **Direct buffer access:** the bytes are read through a raw pointer into `values`, without indexing the vector each time.
- **Fast path:** on little-endian targets, when the field's byte size equals `sizeof(T)`, a single `memcpy` replaces the loop. It produces the same bytes the shift loop would assemble.
- **Other cases:** these fall back to the original loop with the same type conversions, so the results do not change.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T FieldBase::GetValue(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex) const
{
    const FIT_UINT8 typeNum = GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK;
    const FIT_UINT8 size = baseTypeSizes[typeNum];
    if (fieldArrayIndex >= (FIT_UINT8)values.size())
    {
        return *reinterpret_cast<const T*>(baseTypeInvalids[typeNum]);
    }

    const auto* data = values.data() + (fieldArrayIndex * size);

#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && defined(__ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
    if (size == sizeof(T))
    {
        T rv;
        std::memcpy(&rv, data, sizeof(T));
        return rv;
    }
#endif

    T rv = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        rv |= static_cast<T>(data[i]) << (i * 8);
    }

    return rv;
}
```

